import sys
from pathlib import Path
sys.path.insert(0,str(Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]/'1.1'))
from authoring_support import Topic
t=Topic('3.5')
for local,texts in {
 '01':['Perfect competition and monopoly are models with different conditions affecting a firm’s pricing and output choices.','A lower production cost does not establish a lower consumer price or a universal advantage of one market structure.'],
 '01-M01':['Perfect competition is a model with many price-taking firms selling an identical product, with free entry and exit and adequate information.','A competitive firm takes the market price and chooses output with regard to its costs rather than choosing any desired selling price.','In the model, entry in response to abnormal profit can increase market supply and reduce that profit over time.'],
 '01-M02':['Monopoly is a market structure with a single supplier in the defined market and barriers that restrict entry.','A monopolist’s chosen price and quantity are constrained by market demand, costs and any applicable rules.','Scale economies can reduce long-run average cost, but consumers benefit from lower prices only if an appropriate part of the advantage is passed on.'],
}.items():
 for i,text in enumerate(texts):t.claim(local,text,'definition' if i==0 and '-M' in local else 'causal')
t.block('01','comparison','Compare models using their assumptions',"""
Perfect competition and monopoly describe conditions affecting a firm’s pricing and output decisions. They are not descriptions of private versus government ownership. Compare the number of suppliers, the product, entry conditions and the firm’s relationship with demand. A statement about one feature does not settle every outcome. Cost, demand, quality and the objective used to judge performance remain important.

Use invented alternative teaching cases for the same basic product. One has many suppliers of an identical product, adequate information and free entry. The other has one supplier protected by an entry barrier. These assumptions isolate the market structures; they are not claims that a named Namibian industry exactly matches either model. An actual judgement would need evidence about the relevant market rather than assume that a large firm is automatically its only supplier.
""")
t.block('01-M01','definition','Perfect competition and the price-taking firm',"""
Perfect competition is a model with many price-taking firms selling an identical product, with free entry and exit and adequate information. An individual firm is too small relative to the market to determine its price. Asking more than the market price for an identical product gives informed buyers an opportunity to purchase from other suppliers. The firm therefore takes the market price and chooses an appropriate output in relation to its costs.

At that price, an additional unit adds the price to total revenue, so the firm’s marginal revenue equals price in the model. Producing additional units is worthwhile for a profit objective while the extra revenue exceeds the extra cost. For an interior smooth profit maximum, marginal revenue equals marginal cost. Immediately below that output, extra revenue should exceed extra cost; immediately above it, extra cost should exceed extra revenue. This condition describes a maximum rather than any equality. Also compare feasible alternatives and check whether the firm is making a loss. With discrete units, compare total profit and whether the next unit adds more cost than revenue.

Abnormal profit means a return above economic costs including normal profit. Normal profit is the minimum reward needed to retain entrepreneurship in its current use, treated as part of economic cost. Where entry is genuinely free, abnormal profit can attract additional suppliers, expanding market supply and putting pressure on price and profit. This is a model mechanism, not a guarantee that every real firm immediately earns an identical return.
""")
t.block('01-M02','definition','Monopoly and the demand constraint',"""
Monopoly is a market structure with a single supplier in the defined market and barriers that restrict entry. A monopolist faces the market demand relationship rather than the same price-taking position as a small competitive firm. If it wants to sell additional output along a downward-sloping demand curve, it generally needs a lower price. It cannot independently choose any high price and any high quantity without considering buyers’ response.

Marginal revenue is the extra revenue from another unit, distinct from average revenue, which is revenue per unit. When selling more requires lowering the uniform price on the output sold, the extra revenue can be less than the new price because receipts on earlier units are also affected. This helps explain why the monopolist considers both demand and costs when choosing output. Use the profit-maximising comparison of additional revenue and cost under the stated conditions, then read the price buyers will pay for that output from demand.

Monopoly does not guarantee profit. Demand can be weak or costs high, and a barrier to entry does not remove those constraints. A larger operation may obtain economies of scale, reducing long-run average cost. That potential efficiency gain must be distinguished from what consumers receive. A lower cost can increase the firm’s margin without necessarily leading to a lower selling price.
""")
t.block('01-M01 01-M02','evaluation','Separate firm efficiency from consumer outcomes',"""
Competitive pressure can limit an individual firm’s pricing power and encourage attention to cost. Against this, a collection of small firms may lack a particular scale advantage available to one larger supplier. Monopoly can therefore have a cost advantage in a suitable case, while restricted entry can weaken pressure to pass savings to buyers. Neither structure is established as best from its label alone.

Use invented figures: competitive suppliers have average cost N$9 and sell at N$10, while a monopolist’s scale advantage gives average cost N$6 but its price is N$12. The monopolist uses fewer stated cost resources per unit, yet consumers pay more in this comparison. The data do not support calling its lower cost a consumer price saving. If it instead passed sufficient savings through a lower price while maintaining the required quality, the consumer comparison could change. A justified conclusion identifies the beneficiary, the cost conditions and the actual price/output policy.
""")
t.block('01','cross_link','The topic’s ownership boundary',"""
Topic 5.3 develops costs and revenue; topic 5.4 develops economies and diseconomies of scale. Use those ideas here to explain firm behaviour without adding extra market-structure theories. Topic 6.2 owns government economic policy, which is a different meaning of policy from a firm’s pricing and output choices. Define the model, trace the mechanism and then evaluate its consequences for the stated group.
""")
def s(local,sub):return next(x for x in t.wo['item_slots'] if x['objective_id']==t.oid(local) and x['subtype']==sub)
t.card(s('01','FEATURE'),'01','Identify two differences between perfect competition and monopoly concerning suppliers and entry.','Perfect competition has many suppliers and free entry; monopoly has a single supplier in the defined market and barriers restricting entry.',['Feature/contrast (1): many suppliers versus one. Distinction (1): free versus restricted entry.'])
t.card(s('01-M01','DEF'),'01-M01','Define perfect competition.','A model with many price-taking firms selling an identical product, with free entry and exit and adequate information.',['Meaning/definition and essential element (1): the complete model conditions, including price taking, identical product and entry/information assumptions.'])
t.card(s('01-M02','DEF'),'01-M02','Define monopoly.','A market structure with a single supplier in the defined market and barriers that restrict entry.',['Meaning/definition and essential element (1): single supplier in the defined market protected by entry barriers.'])
t.card(s('01-M01','EVAL'),'01-M01','Evaluate perfect competition for consumers where its model assumptions hold, but a larger single producer could obtain genuine scale savings.','Many price-taking suppliers and informed choice constrain individual pricing power. However, smaller suppliers may lack the stated scale savings. Prefer the competitive outcome if its price, quality and cost discipline serve consumers better; if a larger supplier passes on sufficient savings while preserving quality, that alternative may improve the comparison. The structure alone does not settle the outcome.',['Argument for (2): alternative suppliers/information constrain pricing power (1), consumers can switch from an overpriced identical product (1). Argument against (2): small scale may miss savings (1), raising possible cost relative to a suitable larger producer (1). Condition (1): actual prices/quality and passed-on savings. Judgement (2): conditional preference for competitive consumer outcome (1), recognise a changed price/cost comparison could alter it (1). Total 7.'])
t.card(s('01-M02','EVAL'),'01-M02','Evaluate a monopolist’s lower production cost for consumers. Invented comparison: competitive suppliers have average cost N$9 and price N$10; monopoly has average cost N$6 and price N$12 because it passes little of its scale saving to buyers.','The monopoly’s scale saving reduces stated average cost, offering a potential efficiency benefit. However, buyers pay N$12 instead of N$10, so the actual price comparison is worse for them despite lower cost. Judge the cost gain separately from consumer benefit; consumers benefit if enough savings are passed on with quality maintained, which the stated price does not demonstrate.',['Argument for (2): lower average cost N$6 versus N$9 (1), potential efficiency gain from scale (1). Argument against (2): price N$12 versus N$10 (1), savings not sufficiently passed to consumers (1). Condition (1): actual pass-through and quality. Judgement (2): no demonstrated consumer price gain here (1), conditional gain only if price/quality comparison improves (1). Total 7.'])
p=t.wo['performance_tasks'][0]
t.card(p,'01-M01 01-M02','Define perfect competition (2). Identify two monopoly characteristics (2). Explain why an individual competitive firm is a price taker under the model assumptions (2). Identify two constraints on monopoly pricing (2).','Perfect competition has many price-taking firms supplying an identical product under free entry/exit and adequate information. Monopoly has a single supplier in the defined market and entry barriers. An overpriced competitive firm can lose buyers to informed alternatives, limiting its individual pricing power. Market demand and production costs constrain the monopolist.',['Meaning (2): many price-taking firms/identical product (1), free entry/exit and information assumptions (1). Separate points for monopoly (2): single supplier (1), entry barrier (1). Explanation (2): buyers have informed identical alternatives (1), constraining the firm’s price choice (1). Constraints (2): demand (1), costs (1). Marks allocation 8.'])
p=t.wo['performance_tasks'][1]
t.card(p,'01-M01 01-M02','Invented comparison: Case A meets perfect-competition assumptions. Case B has one supplier and an entry barrier. At the compared output, A has average cost N$9 and price N$10; B has average cost N$6 and price N$12. Describe three structural features distinguishing the cases (6). Explain how pricing and output decisions differ, including the role of demand and additional costs/revenue (7). Discuss whether B’s lower cost establishes that it is better for consumers (7).','A has many suppliers of an identical product, free entry and adequate information; B has a single supplier and restricted entry. A firm in A takes the market price and selects output in relation to costs. B faces market demand, so selling more generally requires a price change and its additional revenue can differ from price. Both consider additional revenue and cost for a profit objective under the relevant maximum conditions. B’s lower cost is a potential efficiency advantage, but buyers pay more in the stated comparison. Consumer benefit depends on price, quality and pass-through, not on cost alone.',['Context/data description (6): many versus single supplier (2); free versus restricted entry (2); informed identical alternatives in A versus absence of another supplier within B’s market (2). Mechanism/explanation (7): competitive price taking (1), informed alternatives constrain deviation (1), additional revenue equals market price in A (1); B faces market demand (1), more output can require lower price (1), additional revenue can be below price (1); output compares additional revenue/cost at a maximum (1). Both sides/judgement (7): B cost N$6 versus N$9 (1), potential efficiency gain (1); B price N$12 versus N$10 (1), worse buyer price despite saving (1); condition pass-through/quality (1); reject automatic consumer benefit (1), reconsider only if actual consumer outcome improves (1). Marks allocation 6+7+7=20.'])
p=t.wo['performance_tasks'][2]
t.card(p,'01-M01 01-M02','Evaluate two alternative monopoly outcomes against perfect competition in separate seven-mark plans. Invented Case 1: a large sole producer obtains scale savings and passes enough on to sell below the competitive comparison price, with quality maintained. Case 2: it obtains the same saving but passes little on, charging above the comparison price. Entry is restricted in both cases. Consider efficiency, buyers’ alternatives and the actual price/output policy.','Case 1: scale savings lower cost and a lower price can benefit consumers, but restricted entry reduces their alternative suppliers. Conditional support rests on the maintained price/quality advantage. Case 2: cost savings benefit the firm potentially, while buyers face a higher price and weak alternative supply. Prefer the competitive comparison for the stated consumer-price objective unless price or quality evidence changes. Both judgements distinguish a productive advantage from its distribution rather than declaring monopoly always good or always bad.',['Case 1 marks (7): argument for lower cost (1), saving reaches consumers through lower price (1); argument against restricted alternatives (1), weak entry pressure (1); condition maintained price/quality gain (1); judgement conditionally favours stated outcome (1), no universal monopoly claim (1). Case 2 allocation (7): argument for potential efficiency saving (1), firm can use lower cost (1); argument against higher buyer price (1), restricted alternatives (1); condition actual price/quality compared with competition (1); judgement favours competitive consumer comparison here (1), conditional on unchanged evidence (1). Total 14.'])
t.finish()
